  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with seasonable temperatures \nthrough midweek, trending to above normal late in the week.\n\nGOES water vapor channels continue to depict a highly amplified\npattern with an upper ridge extending from the lower/mid \nMississippi Valley into the southern Great Lakes, with a trough \nlingering off the East Coast. At the surface, 1035mb high \npressure is centered from SE PA into the northern Delmarva this \nafternoon. Mostly sunny with temperatures in the mid to upper \n60s, with dewpoints generally in the mid to upper 40s. The wind \nremains NE and light for much of the area. However, it is still \ngusting to 20-25 mph along the coasts of SE VA and NE NC.\n\nThe surface high continues to settle southward tonight with the\naxis oriented in vicinity of the VA Piedmont. Given this, there\nis a greater potential for a more widespread frost spreading \neast from the Piedmont to interior SE VA as better decoupling is\nexpected. It is a little concerning that dewpoints have \nlingered in the 40s today. However, temperatures away from the \ncoast have been radiating well in this pattern, and have \ncontinued the same thinking as the previous shift to go a few \ndegrees (or several degrees in some areas) colder than NBM. This\nresults in mid 30s forecast for the Piedmont (locally lower 30s\npossible) and and mid-upper 30s for interior SE VA and the \nlower MD Eastern Shore. A Frost Advisory has been issued for the\nNW Piedmont counties where confidence is highest for 2m \ntemperatures falling to near freezing. \n\nAfter the cold start, turning warmer Monday and Tuesday ahead \nof the next (dry) front, then slightly cooler Wednesday and \nstaying dry. Temperatures warm above average late in the week \nwith high temperatures well into the 70s for most, along with no\nrain chances as moisture is expected to stay offshore off the \nSE US coast. The 25/12z ECMWF ensemble/GEFS chance for \nmeasurable rain (.01\"+) of rain through the end of the month is \nbasically zero aside from some low probs showing up in \nsoutheastern portions of the area later in the week into the \nfollowing weekend from the EC ens. (GEFS has none), but when \nswitching the threshold to 0.1\"+ the EC ens. basically has zero \nprobs. It should be noted that despite the ongoing \ndrought/precipitation deficit across much of the area, none of \nour long-term climate sites will rank among the 10 driest \nOctobers on record (as October has several occurrences with \nmonthly rainfall amounts of less than 0.50\").",
